 brought the cattle herd here, he who became a hero stone, / the victorious-speared young lord's absence lamenting, / with cut and shaved head, experiencing widowhood, distressed / like a woman who has discarded ornaments, / you became desolate-looking, having lost many ornaments!","translation":"Alas! I have indeed lived to see my lord’s great house—whose doors were never shut, whose worn courtyard was filled with endless bowls of bee-swarming toddy and limitless food—lying desolate like a boat stranded on an empty riverbed. Let my eyes go blind! O house, in the past you yielded rich feasts, where the sizzling sound of goat meat poured into boiling ghee was like the heavy breathing of a rutting elephant in a king's wealthy palace, satisfying the hunger of needy strangers. But now, because the young lord of the victorious spear—who drove away the fierce bowmen who captured our cattle, while the hooting owl shrieked, and who was adorned with fragrant karantai flowers resembling a young heifer's udder by skilled men, and who brought back the herd and became a hero stone—is no more, you stand desolate, having lost all your splendor, like a grieving widow with a shaven head who has cast away all her ornaments.","modern":"ஐயோ! கதவுகள் மூடப்படாத என் தலைவனுடைய பெரிய இல்லம், வண்டுகள் மொய்க்கும் கள்ளும் எல்லையற்ற உணவும் வழங்கிய தேய்ந்த வாசலைக் கொண்ட முற்றம், இன்று வற்றிய ஆற்றில் கைவிடப்பட்ட தோணியைப் போலக் கிடக்கக் கண்டேன். என் கண்கள் குருடாகட்டும்! முன்பு, உலகைக் காக்கும் மன்னர்களின் செல்வச் செழிப்புள்ள அரண்மனையில் மதம் பிடித்த யானை பெருமூச்சு விடுவது போல, நெய் உலையில் இட்ட ஆட்டு இறைச்சியின் வதங்கும் சத்தம் கேட்க, வறியவர்கள் வயிறார உண்ணுமாறு நீ வாரி வழங்கினாய். ஆனால் இப்போதோ, பசுக்கூட்டங்களைக் கவர்ந்து சென்ற வில்வீரர்களை, ஆந்தையின் அலறல் ஒலிக்க விரட்டியடித்து, இளம்பசுவின் மடி போன்ற நறிய கரந்தைப் பூக்களைத் தகுதி வாய்ந்தோர் சூட்ட, பசுக்களை மீட்டு வந்து நடுகல்லாக நின்ற வெற்றிவேல் ஏந்திய தலைவன் இல்லாததால், தலைமுடியை மழித்துக் கைம்மை நோன்பு நோற்கும் ஆபரணங்களை இழந்த பெண்ணைப் போல, நீ உன் அழகையெல்லாம் இழந்து பொலிவிழந்து கிடக்கிறாய்!","explanation":"The poet Āvūr Mūlaṅkiḻār is lamenting the death of a young chieftain and addressing his now-desolate house. The chieftain died in a cattle-recovery battle, which belongs to the Karantai tiṇai, and has been memorialized as a hero stone (naṭukal). The poet contrasts the past glory of the house, which was always open and filled with the sounds of cooking meat and flowing toddy for guests, with its current desolate state. The house is compared to a boat stranded on a dry riverbed, and its current bareness is compared to a widow who has shaved her head and discarded her ornaments. The hero's bravery is highlighted by his recovery of the cattle and his adornment with karantai flowers, which are characteristic of the Karantai landscape.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"timing":{"male":[[0.0,0.36],[0.6,1.0],[1.0,1.46],[1.57,1.8],[1.8,2.5],[2.5,2.7],[2.7,3.0],[3.0,3.7],[3.7,4.6],[4.6,5.2],[5.2,5.6],[5.6,6.0],[6.0,6.85],[6.85,7.7],[7.7,7.85],[7.85,8.45],[8.45,8.6],[8.6,9.6],[9.6,9.6],[9.6,10.35],[10.5,10.7],[10.7,11.0],[11.0,11.8],[11.8,12.0],[12.0,12.8],[12.8,13.0],[13.0,14.4],[14.4,14.7],[14.82,15.4],[15.4,15.7],[15.89,16.61],[16.61,16.61],[16.61,16.7],[16.7,17.1],[18.52,19.12],[19.12,19.35],[19.42,19.51],[19.51,19.52],[19.52,19.52],[19.52,19.52],[19.52,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,28.12],[28.12,30.62],[30.62,30.84],[30.84,30.84],[30.84,31.3],[31.3,31.3],[31.3,31.82],[31.82,32.22],[32.22,32.52],[32.52,33.12],[33.12,33.52],[33.52,33.72],[33.72,34.12],[34.12,34.52],[35.57,36.02],[36.02,36.45],[36.45,36.82],[37.22,37.62],[37.62,38.22],[38.22,38.69],[38.69,39.02],[39.02,39.41],[39.41,40.32],[40.32,41.02],[41.02,41.4]],"female":[[0.0,0.6],[0.6,1.1],[1.28,1.46],[1.57,1.8],[1.8,2.7],[2.7,3.0],[3.0,3.3],[3.3,4.76],[4.76,5.0],[5.0,5.7],[5.7,5.96],[5.96,6.6],[6.6,8.9],[8.9,9.38],[9.3